# nvim-docker environment
#
# Copy this file to `.env` (in this same directory) and adjust the values for
# this machine / this developer:
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# `.env` is git-ignored on purpose - it holds per-machine absolute paths.
#
# IMPORTANT: these variables are used for INTERPOLATION into docker-compose.yaml
# only. They do NOT automatically become environment variables inside the
# container. Anything the container itself needs must be listed explicitly under
# `environment:` in docker-compose.yaml.
#
# IMPORTANT: the host paths below must be ABSOLUTE and must not use ${HOME} or
# `~`. The compose commands run under `sudo -E`, and sudo's env_reset can hand
# compose root's HOME instead of yours, which would silently mount the wrong
# directory. Spell the paths out in full.
#
# IMPORTANT: the host paths below are bind-mount SOURCES using compose's short
# volume syntax. If a source does not exist, Docker silently creates it as an
# empty directory (owned by root) instead of failing. If a mount looks empty
# inside the container, check for a typo here first.

# Host directory containing your git repositories.
# Mounted read-only at /home/dev/source-host inside the container.
SOURCE_DIR=/home/albert/source

# Host directory containing your notes.
# Mounted read-only at /home/dev/notes inside the container.
NOTES_DIR=/home/albert/notes

# Host path to the 1Password SSH agent socket. Mounted read-write at
# /home/dev/.1password/agent.sock, which is what SSH_AUTH_SOCK points at inside
# the container (see docker-compose.yaml).
# Linux:
HOST_SSH_AGENT_SOCK=/home/albert/.1password/agent.sock
# macOS (note the spaces in the path - do not quote, compose handles it):
# HOST_SSH_AGENT_SOCK=/Users/albert/Library/Group Containers/2BUA8C4S2C.com.1password/t/agent.sock

# Which container registry to use for the image name.
#   dockerregistry.gouws.org     - read/write, use this when you build and push
#   dockerregistry-ro.gouws.org  - read-only public mirror, use this when you
#                                  only want to pull a prebuilt image
DOCKER_REGISTRY=dockerregistry.gouws.org

# Tag of the image that docker-run.sh starts. `latest` is what you want unless
# you are deliberately pinning to a specific git-SHA tag.
IMAGE_TAG=latest

# Timezone inside the container.
TZ=Pacific/Auckland

# Git identity baked into the image at BUILD time (Dockerfile build args).
# docker-build.sh picks these up from here; its --git-email / --git-name flags
# still override them for a one-off build.
GIT_EMAIL=albert@gouws.org
GIT_NAME=Albert Gouws
